# Regression test: pause()/resume() promote whatever job they touch back to the front
# of current_job()'s search (see get_job's `fg` parameter in global_state.rs) -- so once
# `fg` resumes a paused job and starts waiting on its result, a *second* pause of that
# same job (interactively, a second Ctrl-Z) correctly targets the job again, not fg's
# own wrapper job. That reaches the external process fine (via the job's own registered
# controllers), but fg's own wait used to be a plain, non-pause-aware recv() -- it never
# noticed, and stayed blocked on a value the job's worker thread wouldn't produce again
# until resumed a second time. From the interactive session's perspective this looked
# identical to the original "can't get back to the prompt" bug fg itself was written to
# fix, just one level removed.
#
# pause here plays the exact role Ctrl-Z's SIGTSTP handler does -- both just call
# GlobalState::pause(job_id) -- so this reproduces the real scenario without needing an
# actual interactive terminal.

$job_id := $(cmd sleep 15 &)
pause $job_id

# A second pause, timed to land while `fg` below is already waiting on the (by then
# resumed) job. Uses `schedule ... --once &`, not a plain `{...} &` closure: a closure
# block run via `&` gets its own *nested* job for the block body (see Job::eval), and
# that job can be assigned a low, recently-freed id -- risking exactly the kind of
# accidental id collision this whole file is testing around. `schedule`'s `command`
# runs via a direct Command::eval, not another Job::eval, so it doesn't allocate a job
# of its own; the outer `schedule` job is the only new one.
$repause_job := $(schedule 500ms command={pause $job_id} --once &)

fg $job_id
fg $repause_job

# fg should have returned (without hanging) once the second pause landed, without the
# job ever actually completing -- it should still be sitting Paused, re-registered for
# another fg/bg, not gone.
$status := $($(jobs | where {($id == $job_id)} | select status | $list.collect)[0])
assert ($status == "Paused") "job should still be Paused after fg returned from a second pause"

# Clean up quickly rather than waiting out the rest of the sleep: terminate
# interrupts it almost immediately (see terminate.crush), and fg on it afterwards may
# either return the (now-terminated) result or error, depending on exactly when the
# worker thread finishes relative to this -- either is fine, only the earlier assert
# above is this test's actual point.
terminate $job_id
try {
    $(fg $job_id)
} catch {|$e|
}
